# WaveRider Project Rules for Cascade AI
# Rename this file to: .windsurfrules (remove .md extension)
# These rules guide AI behavior to maintain consistency and prevent unwanted changes

## CRITICAL: Data Safety
- NEVER use DROP TABLE in migrations - always use ALTER TABLE
- ALWAYS backup database before running migrations (python backup_database.py)
- NEVER delete data without explicit user confirmation
- Check for destructive SQL commands (DROP, DELETE, TRUNCATE) before suggesting migrations

## UI/Styling Guidelines - READ THIS CAREFULLY ⚠️
- Use existing Tailwind CSS classes - DO NOT create custom CSS unless explicitly asked
- Match the current design system - professional, clean, financial dashboard aesthetic
- Color scheme: Blues and grays for primary UI, gold/yellow for highlights (FIR line)
- NEVER change styling unless user specifically asks for visual changes
- Keep consistent spacing and layout patterns across all pages
- If user says "fix the button" - fix functionality, NOT the styling
- DO NOT add fancy animations, gradients, or "modern" effects without permission

## Component Standards
- Use existing UI components from /components/ui/ (shadcn/ui library)
- Follow established component patterns already in the codebase
- DO NOT create new component libraries or design systems
- Maintain consistent button styles, card layouts, and form patterns
- If a component exists, use it - don't create a new one

## Code Style & Conventions
- TypeScript for all frontend code
- Python for all backend code
- Use existing service patterns (e.g., revenueKPIGenerator.ts, kpiRecordsService.ts)
- Follow existing naming conventions (camelCase for JS/TS, snake_case for Python/DB)
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Add comments for complex logic only

## Architecture Rules - DO NOT CHANGE
- Frontend: React + TypeScript + Vite
- Backend: FastAPI + Python
- Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL)
- Auth: Clerk (working, don't touch)
- State: React Context API (revenue-context.tsx, auth-context.tsx)
- DO NOT introduce new frameworks or major libraries without discussion
- DO NOT suggest "upgrading" to Redux, MobX, or other state management

## System-Level Reasoning & Architecture Awareness

- Always think about the entire WaveRider CFO system, not just a single file:
  - Frontend: `project/` (React + TS + Vite + Tailwind + Clerk)
  - Backend: `backend/` (FastAPI + Supabase PostgreSQL + Clerk)
  - Database: Supabase tables listed below in this file
- Before changing code, do a quick mental pass of:
  - Which frontend pages (`project/src/pages/*Page.tsx`) are involved
  - Which shared components (`project/src/components/`) might be affected
  - Which API routes (`backend/api/*.py`) and database tables are touched
- Always assume:
  - Frontend data flows from Supabase via backend APIs (or existing client utilities)
  - All business logic must remain consistent across pages (LER, FIR, KPIs, bonus rules)
- Prefer reusing existing services and patterns:
  - Example: revenue and KPI logic should reuse `revenueKPIGenerator.ts`, `kpiRecordsService.ts`, etc.
- If a change would affect multiple pages or features, explicitly say:
  - "This change impacts: [list pages/components/APIs]. Here's what might break."

  ## Engineering Workflow – Plan First, Then Apply
- For every user request, follow this workflow:

1) Clarify and Restate
   - Restate the goal in 1–3 bullets in plain English.
   - If something is ambiguous, propose 1–2 reasonable interpretations and clearly state which one you will use.

2) Plan Only (NO CODE CHANGES YET)
   - List all files you intend to touch, with full paths (e.g., `project/src/pages/BusinessIntelligencePage.tsx`).
   - For each file, describe the precise change in 1–2 bullets.
   - Call out any shared components, hooks, or API routes that might affect other pages.
   - Identify any risk areas, such as:
     - Shared UI components used in multiple places
     - Shared KPI/revenue logic
     - Database queries or schema usage
   - STOP here and wait for explicit user approval before writing or changing any code.

3) Apply Changes (AFTER USER APPROVAL)
   - Only modify the files listed in the approved plan.
   - Keep edits as small and focused as possible.
   - Follow existing patterns for:
     - API calls (Zod validation, fetch helpers)
     - React components (hooks → effects → handlers → render)
     - FastAPI routes (Pydantic models, `get_current_user`, user_id filtering)
   - Do NOT add extra refactors or "cleanup" beyond the approved plan.

4) Report Back
   - List all files changed and summarize the change for each file in one bullet.
   - Provide a simple testing checklist the user can follow, for example:
     - "1) Go to /business-intelligence
        2) Select month and year
        3) Confirm KPI X shows Y when data exists
        4) Confirm the page handles empty data without crashing"
   - Mention any potential side effects or follow-up tasks in plain language.

5) Multi-File Impact Checks
   - Whenever you change:
     - Shared components in `project/src/components/` 
     - Shared hooks in `project/src/hooks/` 
     - Shared services in `project/src/api/` or `project/src/utils/` 
     - Backend routes in `backend/api/*.py` 
   - First, search and list where those items are used in the codebase.
   - Explicitly consider and describe what might break in those other locations.

## Data Flow - Single Source of Truth
- FIR targets: revenue_entries.desired_revenue (calculated by calculateMonthlyFIRTargets)
- User ID: Clerk user ID (e.g., user_33fQP5vCktD5cLZwkg7fbysz2JS)
- All pages must use the same data source
- NO custom goal overrides (we removed that feature intentionally)
- KPIs calculate from actual database data, not hardcoded values
- Master Revenue page is the source of truth for FIR targets
- No hardcoded values or dates, all data must come from the database

## What NOT to Change Without Permission
- Database schema (always ask first and create safe migration)
- Authentication flow (Clerk integration is working perfectly)
- Core business logic (FIR calculations, KPI generation)
- File structure (don't reorganize folders without asking)
- Existing working features (if it ain't broke, don't fix it)
- Color schemes or visual design
- Component library (shadcn/ui is our standard)

## When User Says "Fix X" - STAY FOCUSED
- Fix ONLY X - nothing else
- Don't refactor surrounding code "while you're at it"
- Don't change styling "to make it look better"
- Don't add features that weren't requested
- Don't reorganize files or folders
- Ask "Should I also..." if you think something else needs fixing

## Red Flags - STOP AND ASK USER FIRST
- Changing more than 3 files for a simple fix
- Adding new npm/pip packages
- Modifying database schema
- Changing authentication logic
- Introducing new design patterns
- Refactoring working code
- Changing CSS/styling for non-visual bugs
- Suggesting "while we're at it, let's also..."

## Testing & Verification
- Verify TypeScript compiles before suggesting changes
- Check that database queries match actual schema
- Test that changes don't break existing functionality
- Consider edge cases (empty data, missing fields, etc.)
- Don't assume - check the actual code

## Communication Style
- Be concise and direct (user prefers brevity)
- **NO EMOJIS** - User hates emojis, never use them in code, comments, or messages
- Explain WHY you're making changes
- Ask for clarification if requirements are unclear
- Warn about potential breaking changes
- Show before/after for significant changes
- Don't over-explain simple changes

