## Known Issues & Patterns to Avoid
- Don't process years before 2024 (dummy data causes KPI generation to hang)
- Don't re-enable automatic KPI refresh modals (causes infinite loops)
- Don't add Edit Goal feature back (intentionally removed for UX reasons)
- Column is 'period' not 'kpi_period' in kpi_records table
- Use .maybeSingle() not .single() for Supabase queries (prevents 406 errors)
- FIR targets auto-sync on page load (don't suggest manual sync)

## Database Tables & Schema
- revenue_entries: user_id (TEXT), year, month, actual_revenue, desired_revenue, profit_margin, owner_draws
- kpi_records: user_id (TEXT), kpi_name, kpi_value, period (not kpi_period!), target_value, status, goal_value
- financial_documents: user_id (TEXT), document metadata
- services: user_id (TEXT), service definitions
- employee_info: user_id (TEXT), employee data

## Common User Requests & How to Handle
- "Fix the KPI dashboard" → Check data loading, don't change styling
- "The chart isn't showing" → Debug data flow, don't redesign chart
- "Add a feature" → Implement exactly what's asked, nothing more
- "This is broken" → Fix the bug, don't refactor the file
- "Make it look better" → NOW you can suggest styling changes

## When You're Unsure
- ASK the user instead of guessing
- Show options: "I can do A or B, which do you prefer?"
- Explain trade-offs: "This approach is faster but less flexible"
- Admit uncertainty: "I'm not sure about X, let me check the code"
- Don't hallucinate features or APIs that don't exist

## Remember - Core Principles
1. **User has been building this for months** - respect existing patterns
2. **If something looks "old" or "could be better"** - user is willing to listen to suggestions but otherwise, leave it alone unless asked
3. **Consistency > Perfection** - match what's there, don't "improve" it
4. **Working code > "Best practices"** - don't break things to follow trends
5. **User's vision > AI's assumptions** - they know their business better than you
6. **Focus > Feature creep** - do what's asked, nothing more
7. **Safety first** - backup before destructive operations
8. **Ask > Assume** - when in doubt, ask the user

## Success Metrics
- User says "perfect, that's exactly what I needed"
- Changes work on first try
- No unintended side effects
- Styling matches existing design
- User doesn't have to say "no, not like that"
- Code is maintainable and follows existing patterns

## Failure Patterns to Avoid
- "Why did you change the styling? I didn't ask for that"
- "This looks nothing like the rest of the app"
- "You broke something that was working"
- "I just wanted X fixed, why did you change Y and Z?"
- "This is way more complicated than it needs to be"
- "Where did all my data go?" (from DROP TABLE)

